WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.259
Movies sometimes languish in development hell
for years before making it to the big screen,

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.260
and many more end up canceled somewhere along
the way.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:12.460
This holds true even at Disney, where the
costs of traditional or computer animation

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.800
can spell doom for a troubled production.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:19.360
Here's a look at some of the most intriguing
projects Disney has shelved over the years

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.600
and the reasons why audiences are unlikely
to ever see them.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:24.180
Gigantic

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:29.180
Following the Disney tradition of drawing
from classic folk stories, Gigantic was planned

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.390
as a retelling of the English fairy tale "Jack
and the Beanstalk."

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:35.780
This wouldn't have been the first time that
Disney had tackled the classic story.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:41.560
In 1947, the studio released the war-era package
film Fun and Fancy Free.

00:00:41.579 --> 00:00:46.489
That movie included two musical animated shorts,
one of which was the popular Mickey and the

00:00:46.489 --> 00:00:47.000
Beanstalk.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.920
You remember that one, right?

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:50.960
Where Donald Duck tries to slaughter 
a cow?

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.860
"Nice bossy bossy bosssy…."

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:59.060
"Nice ole cowsey wowsey"

00:00:59.200 --> 00:00:59.760
DONALD!

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.700
They don't make em like they used to, eh?

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.980
Wowzer… anyway…

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:11.060
Gigantic was announced with fanfare at the
2015 D23 Expo, and it seemed the project was

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:16.869
already well underway; Tangled director Nathan
Greno was on board to lead the film's development,

00:01:16.869 --> 00:01:19.640
later joined by Inside Out screenwriter Meg
LeFauve.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:24.560
Husband-and-wife songwriting team Robert Lopez
and Kristen Anderson-Lopez were to write the

00:01:24.569 --> 00:01:28.700
score no doubt hoping they could recreate
the magic and commercial success they captured

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:30.860
with the Oscar-winning Frozen soundtrack.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:36.520
Originally slated for a 2018 release, Disney
announced in April 2017 that they were pushing

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:41.710
Gigantic back to 2020 giving the Wreck-It
Ralph sequel the 2018 slot instead.

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:45.360
Apparently, even two extra years of development
time weren't enough.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:50.420
Just six months later, Walt Disney and Pixar
Animation Studios president Ed Catmull told

00:01:50.429 --> 00:01:55.909
Variety that Disney was "ending active development"
on Gigantic instead focusing on a new and

00:01:55.909 --> 00:01:58.960
unspecified project set for Thanksgiving 2020.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:00.760
King of the Elves

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:05.880
Legendary science fiction author Philip K.
Dick's novels and short stories have long

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:10.890
been a favorite of Hollywood studios Blade
Runner, A Scanner Darkly, Total Recall, and

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:13.600
Minority Report were all based on his written
works.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.780
Disney jumped on the bandwagon in 2008, when
they announced that they were planning on

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:22.540
creating an animated feature based on The
King of the Elves, a fantasy short story written

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:24.400
by Dick in 1953.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:30.080
King of the Elves was originally slated for
a late 2012 release, but it's unclear exactly

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:31.460
how far production got.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:35.920
Disney Animation Studios later removed the
King of the Elves page from their website,

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.520
so it's pretty safe to assume that the movie
has been shelved indefinitely.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:40.840
Newt

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.190
Disney revealed a number of their upcoming
projects in a 2008 press release, including

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:50.010
the proposed Pixar animated films The Bear
and the Bow and Newt.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:54.880
While The Bear and the Bow eventually found
its way to the big screen in 2012 as Brave,

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.840
the production of Newt was much more troubled.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.250
Originally planned for a 2011 release, Newt
was to tell the tale of the last remaining

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:06.920
male and female blue-footed newts, forced
together by hopeful scientists in order to

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:10.680
save their species only to discover that they
can't stand each other.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:15.960
If that plot sounds familiar, that's because
it's essentially the storyline found in the

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:18.360
2011 Fox animated feature Rio

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.980
Wait... wwww did you actually think we were going to kiss?

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:23.660
no... no no no.  We just met!

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.580
oh my!  I think they need a little help.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:31.680
After finding out that Fox had beaten them
to the box office with the very similar tale,

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.640
Disney-Pixar canned their plans for Newt.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:39.780
In a 2011 interview, John Lasseter, then-
the company's Chief Creative Officer, acknowledged

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:43.420
that the parallels between the two films were
behind Newt's cancellation.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:45.640
Where the Wild Things Are

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:50.620
Speaking of John Lasseter, in the '80s when
he was just an animator at Disney, he helped

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:53.880
explore the new technology of computer-drawn
animation.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:58.569
At the time, Disney owned the rights to Maurice
Sendak's classic children's book Where the

00:03:58.569 --> 00:04:01.400
Wild Things Are, and had been considering
adapting it.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:06.060
Lasseter began working on a short CGI featurette
designed to be a test of the burgeoning new

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:07.180
computer technology.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:11.840
At the time, though, executives at Disney
weren't interested in using computer technology

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:14.760
unless it could make the production process
faster or cheaper.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:19.100
Disney eventually lost the rights to Sendak's
book, and the whole project came to a halt.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:20.660
Wild Life

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:25.610
You may have heard of George Bernard Shaw's
play Pygmalion, but you're likely more familiar

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:31.000
with some of the popular adaptations of the
tale like 1964's My Fair Lady starring Audrey

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.720
Hepburn, or the always classy take presented
in 1999's She's All That.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:39.520
"Hey now check out the bobos on superfreak."

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:43.580
Then again, My Fair Lady did have this scene

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:48.600
"Come on Gober, move your bloomin' Arse!"

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:56.900
In the early 2000s, Disney's "Secret Lab"
developed their own version of Shaw's tale

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:59.340
an animated feature film called Wild Life.

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:04.250
The details of the proposed plot are sketchy,
but the movie was to be a modern interpretation,

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:08.860
in which an elephant named Ella becomes an
instant celebrity in the New York City club

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:09.360
circuit.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:14.920
Legendary Disney artist Hans Bacher worked
on the ill-fated project for two years, and

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:19.220
he shared some of the concept and character
art for Wild Life on his personal website.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:23.620
Disney eventually pulled the plug because
of story issues and the fact that the dialogue

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:25.840
was peppered with too much adult humor.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:27.380
Yellow Submarine

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:32.900
In the late 2000s, Disney floated plans to
develop a motion-capture remake of Yellow

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:37.940
Submarine, the acclaimed 1968 animated film
based on the music of the Beatles.

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:42.430
Robert Zemeckis was supposed to develop the
remake, but the film experienced numerous

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:43.660
setbacks along the way.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:49.180
Disney finally confirmed the project was underway
in September 2009, but despite all the hype,

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:51.240
The Yellow Submarine would never resurface.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:55.740
Although it hasn't been confirmed, it's been
suggested that Zemeckis' dedication to the

00:05:55.749 --> 00:06:00.620
imperfect motion-capture techniques used in
his previous films like A Christmas Carol,

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:05.520
The Polar Express, and Beowulf probably contributed
to the sinking of Yellow Submarine.

00:06:05.749 --> 00:06:11.099
When Zemeckis' next motion-capture project
Mars Needs Moms flopped spectacularly at the

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:16.100
box-office in 2011, Disney cut both Zemeckis
and his Yellow Submarine project loose.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:18.860
The Nightmare Before Christmas 2

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:24.719
After the unexpected success of The Nightmare
Before Christmas in 1993, Disney was eager

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.160
to capitalize on the film's widespread acclaim.

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:32.080
Originally released under Disney's Touchstone
Pictures banner because of the movie's "spooky"

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:36.809
content, Disney later fully embraced The Nightmare
Before Christmas re-releasing it in theaters

00:06:36.809 --> 00:06:41.810
for several years running and even spending
a bundle to digitize the film for a 3D release

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:42.880
in 2006.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:46.560
But those reissues weren't the only plans
Disney had for the film.

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:51.200
According to director Henry Selick, Disney
started floating ideas for a possible Nightmare

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:53.720
Before Christmas sequel around 2001.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:58.509
Selick claimed that studio execs wanted the
sequel to be CG instead of the stop-motion

00:06:58.509 --> 00:07:00.400
techniques used in the original film.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:05.309
It seems that Tim Burton himself, who produced
and conceived the tale, shut Disney down on

00:07:05.309 --> 00:07:05.980
the sequel talk.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.940
In a 2006 interview with MTV, he explained:

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:13.960
"I was always very protective of [Nightmare],
not to do sequels or things of that kind…it's

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:18.280
not a mass-market kind of thing, it was important
to kind of keep that purity of it."

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:19.880
Tam Lin

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:25.800
After his blockbuster success with The Lion
King in 1994 and his equally successful Broadway

00:07:25.819 --> 00:07:30.619
musical version of the film, director Roger
Allers turned his attention to a new Disney

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:31.120
production.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:33.800
The Kingdom of The Sun was his pet project.

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:37.870
But after conflicts with Disney over the story
and direction of the movie, Allers ended up

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:42.550
making the decision to leave the production
which was retooled and released as The Emperor's

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:44.020
New Groove in 2000.

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:48.560
Allers turned his attention to Lilo and Stitch,
and began to develop a new story pitch based

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:52.030
on the ballad of Tam Lin, a traditional Celtic
folk tale.

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:56.740
Allers developed the pitch, but ultimately
the internal politics at Disney Studios during

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:59.789
this era kept Tam Lin from ever being developed.

00:07:59.789 --> 00:08:00.780
Allers explained:

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:05.640
"I did it for Roy Disney who was eager for
an Irish tale but Michael Eisner, the head

00:08:05.649 --> 00:08:09.870
of the company was in conflict with Roy on
many matters at the time and when I pitched

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:14.740
it to him, he rejected it because of its Irish-ness
[…] Michael Eisner, the CEO of the Disney

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:19.469
company and Roy Disney a leading member of
the Board of Directors and an animation champion

00:08:19.469 --> 00:08:24.119
were engaged in a struggle of whose vision
for the company would prevail, with the result

00:08:24.119 --> 00:08:27.070
that some pictures became casualties if that
struggle."

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:32.050
Although Tam Lin was later picked up by Sony
Pictures Animation in 2003, Allers said the

00:08:32.050 --> 00:08:36.640
team at Sony couldn't agree on how to approach
the movie, and all plans to develop the picture

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:37.400
were scrapped.

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:39.900
The Emperor and the Nightingale

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:44.880
Disney has long turned to the fairytales of
Hans Christian Andersen and others to inspire

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:47.100
their animated and live-action films.

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:51.860
One of these tales, The Nightingale, was first
floated as a possible Disney adaptation in

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:52.980
the 1940s.

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:57.860
Since then, numerous attempts to get The Nightingale
to the big screen have all fallen flat although

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:00.820
Disney did manage to put out a children's
book adaptation.

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:05.380
The Emperor and the Nightingale was reworked
as a computer-animated project in the spring

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:11.000
of 2003, but less than six months later the
project was shelved yet again this time, indefinitely.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:13.200
Who Framed Roger Rabbit Sequel

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:18.960
Director Robert Zemeckis took his Yellow Submarine
remake defeat in stride, and turned his attention

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:22.140
to developing a sequel to Who Framed Roger
Rabbit for Disney.

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:26.460
"Remember me, Eddie?"

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:32.220
Zemeckis seemed to have both Disney and original
Roger Rabbit star Bob Hoskins on board for

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:33.060
his sequel.

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:37.800
But then Hoskins was forced to retire from
acting due to his ill health and sadly passed

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:42.920
away in 2014 which seemed to spell the end
for any talk of a Roger Rabbit sequel.

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:48.020
Never one to admit defeat, Zemeckis claimed
in 2016 that he had a "terrific" script in

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:52.620
place, saying: "It would be very hard to do
but we would do a digital Bob Hoskins."

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:58.100
Before you get too worried about what Robert
Zemeckis plans to do to Hoskins' legacy, rest

00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:02.520
assured that it's extremely unlikely Disney
will ever make this picture and Zemeckis knows

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:03.020
it.

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:07.620
He admitted: "The current corporate Disney
culture has no interest in Roger, and they

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:09.520
certainly don't like Jessica at all."

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:13.580
"I'm not bad...I'm just drawn that way."

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:16.060
Sure you're not, Jessica.

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:17.540
You keep telling yourself that.

